      <!-- 9. DOWN PAYMENT IMPACT -->
      <h2>How a Down Payment Affects Your Car Loan</h2>
      <p>
        Paying a larger upfront down payment directly reduces the amount of money you must borrow from the financier. A higher down payment can provide significant advantages:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reduced Borrowing Amount:</strong> Lowers the initial loan principal balance immediately.</li>
        <li><strong>Lower Monthly EMI:</strong> Since interest is calculated on a smaller balance, your required monthly installment decreases.</li>
        <li><strong>Lower Lifetime Interest:</strong> Reduces the compounding interest charges across the entire life of the loan.</li>
        <li><strong>Protection Against Depreciation:</strong> Vehicles depreciate quickly in the first 2–3 years; a solid down payment (15% to 20%) prevents you from owing more than the vehicle's market value.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 italic">
        * Financing decisions depend on personal finances, emergency savings reserves, and loan terms. This information does not constitute personalized financial advice.
      </p>

      <!-- 10. PRINCIPAL VS INTEREST -->
      <h2>Principal vs Interest in a Car Loan</h2>
      <p>
        In an amortizing auto loan, every monthly payment is divided into two distinct components:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Principal:</strong> The actual borrowed money that goes directly toward paying off the vehicle purchase balance.</li>
        <li><strong>Interest:</strong> The finance fee charged by the lender for providing the loan.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because interest is calculated on your remaining unpaid loan balance at the beginning of each monthly cycle, your early monthly installments consist of a higher percentage of interest. As you gradually repay the principal, the interest portion decreases each month, allowing a greater share of your EMI to pay down the remaining principal.
      </p>

      <!-- 11. COMMON MISTAKES -->
      <h2>Common Mistakes When Using a Car Loan Calculator</h2>
      
      <h3>Looking only at the monthly EMI</h3>
      <p>
        Focusing solely on securing a low monthly payment can tempt buyers into 72 or 84-month loan terms. While the monthly payment looks attractive, the total interest paid over 7 years can add tens of thousands of rupees to the vehicle's true cost.
      </p>

      <h3>Forgetting taxes and fees</h3>
      <p>
        Vehicle showroom prices often exclude road taxes, registration charges (RTO), documentation fees, and dealer processing costs. If these costs are added to your loan balance, your financing principal increases.
      </p>

      <h3>Ignoring the down payment</h3>
      <p>
        Opting for zero down payment or minimal cash down leads to higher borrowing costs and increases the chance of having negative equity if you decide to sell or trade in the car before the loan ends.
      </p>

      <h3>Ignoring total interest</h3>
      <p>
        The monthly payment alone does not show the complete borrowing cost. Always inspect the total interest figure and total out-of-pocket expenditure when evaluating loan options.
      </p>

      <h3>Not checking refinance costs</h3>
      <p>
        When refinancing, failing to account for prepayment penalties on your existing loan or upfront processing charges on the new loan can erase expected savings.
      </p>

      <h3>Treating the estimate as a final loan offer</h3>
      <p>
        Calculator figures are mathematical estimates based on user inputs. Final approved interest rates, processing fees, and loan terms depend on formal lender underwriting, income verification, and credit bureau scores.
      </p>

      <!-- 12. IMPORTANT ASSUMPTIONS AND DISCLAIMER -->
      <h2>Important Assumptions and Information</h2>
      <p>
        Please keep the following guidelines in mind when reviewing your car loan calculation results:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Standard Amortization Model:</strong> Calculations utilize the standard monthly reducing balance amortizing formula.</li>
        <li><strong>Estimates Only:</strong> Results are mathematical estimates and do not represent a guaranteed loan approval, official sanction letter, or binding quote.</li>
        <li><strong>Interest Rates & Eligibility:</strong> Actual interest rates and loan sanctions depend on your credit profile, monthly income, vehicle age, and lender policies.</li>
        <li><strong>Taxes & Insurance:</strong> Insurance premiums, annual road taxes, maintenance costs, and accessory charges are excluded unless explicitly entered into the calculator.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepayment Rules:</strong> Prepayment calculations assume that extra payments are applied directly to principal reduction without prepayment penalty restrictions. Verify your lender's part-prepayment terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Independent Platform:</strong> This calculator is an independent financial education tool and does not sell loans or represent any specific bank or automobile brand.</li>
      </ul>

    </section>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- FAQ SECTION SLOT (EXACTLY 7 FAQs AS REQUIRED) -->
